.NOT. binds before every comparison operator, so `.NOT. sCode = "AB"`
is `(.NOT. sCode) = "AB"` (diag.parenthesize_negated_comparison, warning,
on the whole comparison). A string or number literal under the .NOT.
always raises, so that form is an error. Parenthesized comparisons and an
explicitly grouped negation stay silent (issue #339).
-- input.ssl --
:PROCEDURE CheckCode;
	:PARAMETERS sCode, nLimit, bReady;
	:DECLARE bOut;
	bOut := .NOT. sCode = "AB";
	bOut := .NOT. nLimit < 3;
	bOut := .NOT. 5 == nLimit;
	bOut := .NOT. (sCode = "AB");
	bOut := (.NOT. bReady) == .F.;
	bOut := .NOT. bReady .AND. bOut;
	:RETURN bOut;
:ENDPROC;
-- format --
:PROCEDURE CheckCode;
	:PARAMETERS sCode, nLimit, bReady;
	:DECLARE bOut;

	bOut := .NOT. sCode = "AB";
	bOut := .NOT. nLimit < 3;
	bOut := .NOT. 5 == nLimit;
	bOut := .NOT. (sCode = "AB");
	bOut := (.NOT. bReady) == .F.;
	bOut := .NOT. bReady .AND. bOut;

	:RETURN bOut;
:ENDPROC;
-- diagnostics --
4:10-4:28 warning parenthesize_negated_comparison: '.NOT.' binds before '=', so this negates the left operand alone - write '.NOT. (a = b)' to negate the comparison
5:10-5:26 warning parenthesize_negated_comparison: '.NOT.' binds before '<', so this negates the left operand alone - write '.NOT. (a < b)' to negate the comparison
6:10-6:27 error parenthesize_negated_comparison: '.NOT.' binds before '==', so it applies to a number literal and raises at run time - write '.NOT. (a == b)' to negate the comparison
-- diagnostics [--strict --info] --
